# Revenue Management
#
# Pricing a perishable room or seat: the measures, the capacity rules from Littlewood onward, rate fences and their fairness, channels and rate parity after the Digital Markets Act, and the derivations still owed.

#>management_revenue
#:layer #!pragmatics
#:domain #!management
#:form #!comparison
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bane

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Revenue Management"
 ~#claim: "Revenue management sells perishable capacity by protecting stock for later high-fare demand and fencing prices by segment; in the EU, gatekeeper platforms may no longer impose rate parity."
 ~#proof: "Littlewood and Belobaba give the protection rules; Kimes and Wirtz test fence fairness; the Digital Markets Act and the 2024 designation of Booking set the parity rule."
 ~#door: "Read the measures, run the two-fare kit, then compare the method rows and their gap cells."
 ~#audience: "hospitality and airline pricing students and analysts"
 continuity: "Revenue Management | perishable capacity"
 cold_open: "An empty room tonight is gone forever, so revenue management prices perishable capacity before demand arrives."
}

